#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124212 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "1a855d24-cb78-5398-89e1-3bfda6907f36" :question/math-academy-id "e-4787" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L,$ we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x).$\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$\n\n- For each $p(x) \\in L,$ we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x) \\in L.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124318 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/id #uuid "57bf5fb6-02e9-56a1-9641-342123f45406" :question/math-academy-id "e-8229" :question/problem "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A= (a_{ij})$ such that $a_{11} + a_{22} > 0.$ Which of the following properties are true?\n\n- For any $A \\in P,$ we have $1 \\cdot A = A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.$\n\n- For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have $(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot a_{ij} = a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta a_{ij}) = (\\alpha\\beta) a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) a_{ij} = \\alpha a_{ij} + \\beta a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "ff4fdeaf-bdb3-54d7-b5e9-2f3ad88b55a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9960" :question/problem "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $I_2 \\in M$\n\n- For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M.$\n\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/191451016703b3287c7e58fdb59e22effb919c97dcad7a9e3bec09efd115795a.png) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/3b13b13c14eb335adb58b3d1ff3132b4e189065033481aaf371a9376188f2443.png) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/a906a8520f0b2f676bcf97411232fa3303f87aec9dcae9abe19dca00ab6da1a7.png)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ff2e01-5e8f-539f-a8de-2c9d6e6e074e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377328} {:answer/id #uuid "902ac293-50fe-5dd3-a103-1eb64f244826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377329} {:answer/id #uuid "36aad3d0-8ae8-5edd-ae55-22e11ce5fe77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377330} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a5a874-ca88-5ffe-9815-2312da44d691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377331} {:answer/id #uuid "b6450ae3-3fe9-5e6e-87f9-8a22693482e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2a5a874-ca88-5ffe-9815-2312da44d691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377331} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca9b6a6-d9cf-5797-968a-b4a9d7b52612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ae15ced-8119-5af5-8ffc-48ebd6ecda50" :question/math-academy-id "q-106688" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4c52d5a-0844-5105-ab53-9eb50de6ede1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121130} {:answer/id #uuid "eb97c9b4-7896-56b3-be8d-9d8f9d2670e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121131} {:answer/id #uuid "ff605ad5-60a7-5783-9696-ad7ac1f6d9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121132} {:answer/id #uuid "38dd97d7-614e-5024-b17b-a142e8e5b743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121133} {:answer/id #uuid "3fefeb7d-9f86-58ee-8a04-8ba57c157f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb97c9b4-7896-56b3-be8d-9d8f9d2670e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121131} :answer-field/id #uuid "97795195-e071-52ca-9410-8f360a4924cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a98e5d99-323b-544b-bce7-36fa3d3ba9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106806" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n- For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For a matrix in $A\\in S,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(2A)=(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2)A=1\\cdot A=A\n$$\n\nsince the scalar multiplication of matrices is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3b8bf9b-3359-58a2-81f8-44ba8f1a56b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322645} {:answer/id #uuid "acce2ad7-4ac7-5970-a743-89273d864945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322646} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4509b7-1334-5a79-9a08-5ad5b05b2e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322647} {:answer/id #uuid "7de40741-cf1e-5c83-89c4-65cbaa869262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322648} {:answer/id #uuid "8239421b-d8d3-5944-91c5-fe78a7c68f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acce2ad7-4ac7-5970-a743-89273d864945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322646} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa95a4ac-6bfb-5e7d-9774-948048284a6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f7c3c39-8a1a-5a06-82f0-07fdf953c652" :question/math-academy-id "q-160861" :question/problem "Let $K$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=-1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in K,$ where $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in K$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in K.$\n- The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbee640b-a95d-5f36-8f8c-3373fc92993b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322652} {:answer/id #uuid "048b116d-bb91-59ae-b961-45ef3fa4b588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee5c590-1042-5faa-b633-57fa5b5a8af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322654} {:answer/id #uuid "00dcddd8-ebfd-5c34-98ec-c9c1418284a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322655} {:answer/id #uuid "c8308f24-2099-5ec8-80b2-960d497009c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "048b116d-bb91-59ae-b961-45ef3fa4b588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653} :answer-field/id #uuid "12eab755-1d62-5455-bb52-18e7f27dab8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15b63522-0c12-5bc3-a318-dae5fc4b32c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160866" :question/problem "Let $W$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in W.$\n- The set $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)=(0)^{2}+1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple not in $W.$ For example, let $p(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $p(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $p(0)=1.$ However, \n\n$$\n2\\cdot p(0)=2\\cdot ({0}^{2}+1)=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n Hence, $k\\cdot p(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ac0766-2fb8-5197-94b9-fa2143aeadc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322687} {:answer/id #uuid "3b7e8965-d181-5f32-8451-0deac8c0cff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322688} {:answer/id #uuid "96f28374-50d2-5eb3-bddf-8e6eb514a5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322689} {:answer/id #uuid "91de564a-48cc-536d-8949-26f7ed45d9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322690} {:answer/id #uuid "01176b20-1014-5569-9e31-47827a7755e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b7e8965-d181-5f32-8451-0deac8c0cff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322688} :answer-field/id #uuid "9544c342-fa35-56f7-bcf9-490b56d4bcca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79a70322-eda4-52ca-9b62-4aa2732797d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-43859" :question/problem "Let $W$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={2}^{x}.$\n- For any $f(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in W.$\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={2}^{x}\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple *not* in $W.$ For example, let $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $f(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $f(0)=1.$ However,\n\n$$\n2\\cdot f(0)=2\\cdot {2}^{0}=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nHence, $k\\cdot f(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "655dccb1-b3b3-5aa5-8f2f-06abb4c197af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322694} {:answer/id #uuid "7df87f18-3990-52c0-bce9-cd0ffa554069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322695} {:answer/id #uuid "2da73cfc-c4fd-5400-bf14-ebf78d6216bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322696} {:answer/id #uuid "5c095616-2c46-5774-b4d5-60c2697ff444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322697} {:answer/id #uuid "ee59f476-3097-5a82-8125-a260dcbb7304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c095616-2c46-5774-b4d5-60c2697ff444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322697} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68bd26e-3789-535f-861e-cdf4cefee6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4de4ed8a-e25a-599c-88bb-6ed0a739caa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94649" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The constant function $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ belongs to $N.$\n- For any $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we have $f(t)-g(t)=g(t)-f(t).$\n- The addition of functions in $N$ is commutative." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $N$ contains only functions that evaluate to zero at zero. However, the function defined by $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ satisfies $f(0)=1\\ne 0.$ So, $f\\notin N.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider functions $f(t)=2t$ and $g(t)=t$ in $N.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)-g(t) & =2t-t \\\\  & =t \\\\ g(t)-f(t) & =t-2t \\\\  & =-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(t)-g(t)\\ne g(t)-f(t).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=g(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of functions is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94b3a172-3584-5ffd-982f-0f3370f6e593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123965} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0af473-719f-51a5-a877-96dcd378fb16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123966} {:answer/id #uuid "eb14b59d-3f60-5be8-9b8f-51c1297ee0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123967} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4d25c4-8d53-58fe-8719-4a9708aef584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123968} {:answer/id #uuid "571c71e7-04c9-571d-b307-95bec7375fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94b3a172-3584-5ffd-982f-0f3370f6e593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123965} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e581fc2-2f3c-59f7-aa50-f3b3371d4f0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d4d76d9-0c38-5edc-a8b9-c85d1bae32b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94694" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $h(x)\\in U,$ where $h(x)=3x.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $U$ contains only polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $h(x)=3x$ is *not* a polynomial of this form. So, $h(x)\\notin U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x)=a{x}^{3}\\in U,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient, and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot p(x)=k(a{x}^{3})=(ka){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real coefficient, we conclude that $(ka){x}^{3}\\in U,$ i.e., $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e9adc8-3918-5315-a6ff-e82c12a245a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377356} {:answer/id #uuid "401b334b-5597-5b5f-82a4-25df094c9535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377357} {:answer/id #uuid "964c4be1-c9d8-5f55-9e39-b6f5c084dc77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377358} {:answer/id #uuid "24fe5ca6-4971-57b6-a136-75713e667059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377359} {:answer/id #uuid "30cf8744-11ec-5ba4-b6a7-c040b81cf95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20e9adc8-3918-5315-a6ff-e82c12a245a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377356} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdc5419d-eb1f-54ff-adcc-5bc70388ef65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe7eb437-c49e-5d72-9bf5-386650b87dda" :question/math-academy-id "q-94723" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2x\\in T.$\n- The scalar multiplication of polynomials in $T$ is associative.\n- For any $p(x)\\in T$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $2x$ is not a polynomial of this form. So, $2x\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8848460-74ea-55ec-b3a5-8170306c2dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123971} {:answer/id #uuid "e4be7317-37b4-5c33-b9fd-5c6ae3b75fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123972} {:answer/id #uuid "1685fa5f-62cf-55f2-ad2c-8231d3a0d4f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123973} {:answer/id #uuid "4e796911-c7db-5ac9-8449-1dcc4c3aeb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123974} {:answer/id #uuid "7ceda5ee-66ab-5a80-9fc2-e4e34c1edc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ceda5ee-66ab-5a80-9fc2-e4e34c1edc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123975} :answer-field/id #uuid "b32ef2aa-8a5c-5372-ab0e-6497823ac9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123970}] :question/id #uuid "bd415fe2-3ead-5cbb-88ba-59d9d1273317" :question/math-academy-id "q-94740" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0, ∞)$ to $R$ of the form $f(x) = a\\log_{2} (x)$, where $a ∈ R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n2. For any $α, β ∈ R$ and $f(x) ∈ L$, we have $(α + β)f(x) = αf(x) + βf(x)$.\n3. For any $α ∈ R$ and any $f(x), g(x) ∈ L$, we have $α(f(x) + g(x)) = αf(x) + αg(x)$."}]}
